.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs commands through a local service, schedules resource reservations,
records output and supervises their lifetime.
The service is
.Xr jobd 8 .
By default the service and its clients run under the same Unix account;
a service may share its socket with one Unix group.
Arguments after the option delimiter execute literally, including a single argument.
Use an explicit shell command such as sh -c, or
.Fl -shell Ar executable
to run that shell with -c, the first command argument as script,
job as its zero argument, and all remaining arguments as positional parameters.
.Fl -legacy-shell
explicitly restores the old single-argument Bash interpretation.
.Pp
.Cm run
streams original stdout and stderr separately while waiting.
Use --summary or --json for a final result view instead.
.Cm submit
prints an identifier and returns immediately.
.Cm wait
waits for completion without replaying output; use
.Fl -summary
to request the diagnostic summary, or
.Fl -json
for a versioned outcome.
.Cm status
reports state, and
.Cm cancel
requests termination of a job accessible through this service.
Session labels are descriptive metadata, not an authorization boundary.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Ss Explicit profiles and classes
.Fl -execution-profile Ar name@revision
on run, submit, create or edit selects an execution profile from service configuration.
.Fl -class Ar name@revision
selects an admission class, which changes no kernel weights.
Queue and Group create/set accept
.Fl -job-execution-profile
and
.Fl -job-class
as inherited selectors.
The value none disables the corresponding inherited selection.
Direct Job fields win over profile defaults; inherited profiles lose to fields
set on the same or a closer collection. Ancestor constraints remain binding.
.Pp
.Cm profile list | show Ar name@revision
and
.Cm class list | show Ar name@revision
inspect accepted definitions with schema version and SHA-256 digest.
Status JSON includes the attempt's resolved preset snapshot.
Accepted revisions are immutable and historical attempts retain their definitions.
See
.Xr job.conf 5
for configuration.
.Ss Admission ordering
.Fl -priority Ar integer
on run, submit, create or edit selects optional admission urgency
from -1000 through 1000; higher values rank first.
It changes no kernel CPU or I/O weight and does not preempt running work.
Queue and Group create/set accept this field as a default, plus
.Fl -priority-min , Fl -priority-max , Fl -aging , Fl -strict-fifo
and
.Fl -backfill .
Unset removes local configuration and reveals inherited values.
.Pp
All ancestor priority bounds intersect; children cannot widen them.
.Cm reprioritize Ar id Fl -priority Ar integer
changes only held or queued work, checks the attempt and records the actor/change.
Original submission is retained.
.Cm explain Ar id Op Fl -json
reports priority, sources, bounds, eligible waiting credit, effective score,
FIFO constraints, backfill and blocking reasons.
Launched attempts retain their policy snapshot.
.Pp
Aging adds one point per complete configured interval of eligible waiting.
Units are ms, s, m, min and h; bare numbers mean seconds.
Durations must be positive whole milliseconds within unsigned 64-bit range.
Held and administratively paused time is excluded; resource waits count.
Credit survives same-boot restart, renames and moves; retry starts at zero.
Reboot retains confirmed credit only.
Changing the interval reinterprets retained credit; disabling aging stops accrual.
Scores have no aging cap; counter overflow stops admission visibly.
.Pp
Strict FIFO accepts true or false.
An ancestor's true takes precedence over urgency and cannot be disabled by children.
Backfill accepts off or conservative.
With off, protected waiters hold later competing reservations or work sharing
a capped ancestor; work without a shared constraint can start independently.
Protection is retained through intermediate waiters with overlapping resources.
Conservative backfill protects planned opportunities across host and ancestor
request budgets and concurrency constraints.
Time limits include termination grace in predictions; unknown durations can block backfill.
Predictions remain advisory: indefinite running work or persistent holds can prevent a start.
An empty Queue enables none of these policies.
.Ss Hierarchical fair share
.Cm group create | set Ar path Fl -fair-share Ar mode
enables a local sibling competition scope.
Modes are cpu-request-time, memory-request-time and off.
CPU mode accounts requested milli-CPU times elapsed milliseconds;
memory mode accounts requested bytes times elapsed milliseconds.
Every descendant Job needs a positive resolved reservation for the selected resource.
.Fl -share-weight Ar integer
on a child Queue or Group accepts 1 through 10000, with effective weight 1
inside a configured parent scope when unset.
.Pp
The least-served weighted child is considered first, recursively,
before Job urgency; strict FIFO and all admission gates still apply.
Descendant Queue counts do not multiply a Group's outer share.
New or rejoining children start at the scope's service watermark,
retain prior debt and earn no unlimited idle credit.
Weights never change kernel CPU or I/O controls.
.Pp
Starting, running, suspended and stopping attempts retain reservations
and accrue accounted service until the service releases them.
Same-boot downtime charges recorded reservations until reconciliation;
reboot retains confirmed history only.
This measures retained reservations, not actual utilization.
The horizon is cumulative participation, with no rolling expiry.
.Pp
Weight changes retain historical normalized service and apply prospectively;
changing a scope's resource basis requires an inactive subtree.
A temporary one-dispatch-interval lookahead spreads simultaneous starts.
.Cm explain
shows scope, child, resource, weight, service, remainder, watermark and active lookahead.
Shares are relative targets, not guaranteed percentages or start times.
Indefinite running work can prevent convergence.
.Ss Pressure observations
.Cm pressure Op Ar id Op Fl -json
reads CPU, memory and I/O pressure from the host, or from the recorded cgroup
of a local active Job attempt.
Each some/full metric reports availability, avg10/avg60/avg300 and total stall time.
JSON averages are integer basis points (100 means one percent);
total_us is an unsigned integer count of microseconds.
The snapshot includes wall time, boot identity/time when available,
source paths and the observed workload cgroup device/inode.
Host CPU full is undefined.
Unavailable measurements have a reason and no numeric substitute.
Watch-backend, remote, unstarted and finished Jobs have no local workload PSI.
.Pp
.Cm pressure parse Ar file Fl -resource Ar cpu|memory|io Op Fl -host
parses saved kernel pressure text and emits JSON without contacting the service.
The host flag marks host CPU full as undefined.
File input is limited to 4096 bytes; final-component symlinks are refused.
Malformed metrics report unavailable while valid metrics remain usable.
.Pp
Observation creates no cgroup and changes no admission policy or kernel control.
It shows affected work, without establishing which workload caused contention.
Snapshots are sequential reads, not atomic samples across resources.
.Pp
.Cm pressure status | events Op Fl -json
emits versioned JSON for current controller rules or the most recent 256 transitions.
The state includes phase, source scope, signal, thresholds, temporary concurrency
ceiling and next reconsideration time on the boot-relative clock.
.Cm queue create | set Ar path Fl -pressure Ar file
and the corresponding Group commands load a JSON array of local pressure rules.
.Cm queue unset Ar path Cm pressure
removes the local policy; ancestor rules still apply.
Rules create no resource domain and observe the maximum selected average
among existing active descendant workload cgroups.
This is an any-descendant predicate, not aggregate PSI.
Service host rules are described in
.Xr job.conf 5 .
.Pp
Sustained high pressure holds new starts, leaving running work unchanged.
After a minimum hold and sustained low pressure, a temporary ceiling starts
at current active concurrency plus one and increases by one per recovery interval.
It clears when current demand fits; all explicit limits remain binding.
Missing data never counts as low pressure.
Required missing metrics hold starts; optional missing metrics leave an open
rule open and preserve any existing restrictive state.
State publication requires a durable journal write.
Sampling uses a nominal one-second interval.
Kernel PSI notifications may trigger earlier measurements, at most one batch
per 250 milliseconds. A two-second notification window uses the rule's high
threshold as a wake hint; admission still uses its configured average and timers.
The service bounds monitors to 256 sources and reports omitted sources,
registration failures and runtime notification counts through pressure status.
Denied monitors fall back to sampling and retry after 30 seconds.
Required monitoring requires measurements, not notification permissions.
.Pp
.Cm pressure replay Ar file Op Fl -json
evaluates a versioned JSON signal trace using the live controller transition logic.
It contacts no service and performs no kernel or service mutation.
The trace contains schema_version 1, host, rule and frames.
Each frame supplies boot_id, at_ms, signal, active and demand,
with optional restart for a same-boot service restart.
Time must increase within a boot; returning to an earlier boot is invalid.
Input is limited to one MiB and 4096 frames; final-component symlinks are refused.
Output contains the state after each frame and whether this rule would hold
one additional start. It does not simulate other scheduler gates or throughput.
.Ss Live resource changes
.Cm update Ar id Op Ar controls
changes a local running or suspended Job's explicit CPU, memory or device I/O controls.
.Cm queue update Ar path
and
.Cm group update Ar path
change an existing shared resource domain and its configuration for future starts.
.Fl -dry-run
previews changes without writing.
Running commands, environments and admission reservations remain unchanged.
.Pp
Every finite reduction of memory.max requires
.Fl -allow-oom ;
it can cause reclaim and terminate processes.
Readback confirms the configured ceiling, not immediate reduction of usage.
Memory.high can throttle work heavily.
Existing ancestor ceilings and OOM grouping remain in force.
.Pp
Updates across controls and devices are not atomic.
The service journals intent, individual writes and metadata publication.
.Cm resource-update Ar operation Op Fl -json
reports progress and errors.
Exit 75 means pending; inspect that operation instead of submitting again.
While pending, new admission and conflicting administration wait;
signals, cancellation, attachment and suspension remain available.
.Fl -abandon
ends an incomplete operation only after its target domain is empty;
completed writes are not rolled back.
Use ordinary set/unset after draining to change domain topology.
.Ss Execution controls
.Bl -tag -width "--allow-write path"
.It Fl -pty
Allocate a persistent terminal before execution.
.Cm run
attaches immediately and requires terminal input and output.
.Cm submit
leaves the terminal detached.
The option cannot be combined with remote execution through
.Fl -on .
.It Fl q Ar name , Fl -queue Ar name
Submit to a named queue.
.It Fl -dir Ar directory
Set the working directory.
.It Fl -label Ar key=value
Attach a label to the Job; repeat it for several.
See
.Sx LABELS .
.It Fl -stdin
Only
.Cm run :
pass the standard input of the client to the Job.
See
.Sx STANDARD INPUT .
.It Fl -on-interrupt Cm forward | detach | cancel
Only
.Cm run
in pipe mode: what the first SIGINT or SIGTERM to the client does.
See
.Sx ATTACHED RUNS AND DETACHED SUBMISSIONS .
.It Fl -pids-max Ar count | Cm unlimited
Limit the number of processes and threads of the Job through the cgroup
control pids.max.
It reserves nothing and needs a delegated pids controller; the value written
is read back and shown as applied_resources in
.Cm status Fl -json .
.It Fl -write-budget Ar size
Stop the Job once it has written more than this many bytes.
The service counts what the Job writes; it sets no file system quota, and
.Cm host Fl -json
reports filesystem_quota as unsupported with the reason.
.Fl -disk
is the earlier spelling.
.It Fl -cores Ar number
Reserve CPU capacity and set its relative CPU weight.
This is not a per-job hard CPU quota.
.It Fl -mem Ar size
Declare a memory reservation and limit, for example 4G.
.It Fl -cpu-request Ar number
Account CPU capacity for admission only, without deriving a weight or quota.
.It Fl -cpu-limit Ar number | unlimited
Set a CPU bandwidth ceiling with a fixed 100000 microsecond period.
The minimum finite value is 0.01 CPU. This does not create a reservation.
.It Fl -cpu-weight Ar number
Set relative kernel CPU weight from 1 through 10000, independently of admission.
.It Fl -memory-request Ar size
Account memory for admission without setting a kernel limit.
.It Fl -memory-high Ar size | unlimited
Set the memory reclaim and throttling threshold.
.It Fl -memory-max Ar size | unlimited
Set the hard memory ceiling.
.It Fl -memory-swap-max Ar size | unlimited
Set the separate swap ceiling; zero disables swap for this workload.
.It Fl -pids Ar number
Earlier spelling: reserve this many processes and threads at admission and
limit the Job to them.
Use
.Fl -pids-max
for a limit without a reservation; giving both with different values is
refused.
.It Fl -disk Ar size
Earlier spelling of
.Fl -write-budget :
limit bytes written, not occupied filesystem space.
.It Fl -time Ar duration
Limit execution time, for example 30m. The time counts from the start of the
command, not from the admission of the Job.
.It Fl -budget Ar duration
Set the ordinary run client's waiting budget; none waits to completion.
PTY attachment lasts until detach, connection loss or job exit.
.It Fl -session Ar label
Record a descriptive session label.
.It Fl -net Cm default | none | socks5://HOST:PORT | http://HOST:PORT | https://HOST:PORT | wireguard:FILE | ns:NAME | profile:NAME
Use host networking, a network namespace with loopback only, a network
namespace whose only way out is the named proxy or WireGuard tunnel, an existing
network namespace that the service configuration names, or a routing profile
that the service configuration defines.
See
.Sx NETWORK BOUNDARIES .
.It Fl -net-secret-file Ar file
Read the proxy's USER:PASSWORD from
.Ar file ,
which must be a regular file of the service user with mode 600.
Only its path is recorded.
.It Fl -bandwidth Ar rate
Cap the Job's network each way, for example 5Mbit; UDP and TCP alike.
.It Fl -confine
Restrict filesystem writes with Landlock.
.It Fl -allow-write Ar path
Add a writable path; implies confinement.
.It Fl -idempotency-key Ar key
With run, submit and create: name this submission.
A later request with the same key and the same specification returns the
Job already recorded and adds none; run then waits for that Job.
The same key with a different specification is refused.
The key is 1 to 128 characters of A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ : - and is free again
once its Job record is removed.
.It Fl -json
Return machine-readable records where supported.
Not available with interactive
.Cm run Fl -pty .
.El
.Pp
Use
.Nm Fl -help
for the full set of options, remote execution, network shaping and queue settings.
.Sh RESOURCE DEFAULTS
.Pp
Explicit new kernel controls require a writable delegated controller.
CPU values use at most three fractional digits; memory inputs must express
whole bytes. Memory thresholds round upward to page boundaries.
Use the same resource names prefixed with
.Fl -job-
in Queue/Group create or set to define per-Job defaults, for example
.Fl -job-cpu-limit .
These defaults are distinct from aggregate collection limits.
.Cm unset
restores inheritance. Changes affect future submissions and explicit edits
or retries, not existing waiting or running attempts.
.Cm status Fl -json
includes resource_sources and applied_resources, separate from the admission
reservation. The original submission remains available.
.Sh AGGREGATE RESOURCE CONTROLS
On a Queue or Group, --cpu-limit, --cpu-weight, --memory-high,
--memory-max and --memory-swap-max apply to the complete subtree.
Options prefixed with --job- provide individual Job defaults instead.
Every ancestor ceiling remains binding; local unlimited cannot escape it.
Empty objects create no additional kernel domains.
.Pp
Use queue/group show --json to inspect all aggregate domains.
Job status records the launch domain chain and workload cgroup path.
Changing active aggregate policies with set/unset is refused.
Finish the affected work before changing those settings.
Remote execution under local aggregate constraints is refused.
.Pp
CPU weights apply among sibling kernel domains and are not host percentages.
Kernel OOM events are recorded without attributing them to an unproven limit.
.Sh DEVICE I/O
.Fl -io-max Ar MAJOR:MINOR,rbps=RATE,wbps=RATE,riops=N,wiops=N
sets device byte rates and IOPS. Omitted directions are unlimited.
Rates use whole bytes per second with binary size suffixes, or unlimited.
IOPS use positive whole numbers, or unlimited.
Repeat the option for distinct devices; duplicate device entries fail.
.Pp
.Fl -io-weight Ar MAJOR:MINOR=WEIGHT
selects enabled IOCost with weights from 1 to 10000.
.Fl -io-bfq-weight Ar MAJOR:MINOR=WEIGHT
selects active BFQ with weights from 1 to 1000 and low_latency=0.
The service does not change device-wide scheduler or cost policies.
.Pp
Use whole-block-device numbers from
.Cm host Fl -json .
Partitions and unavailable backends are refused.
Each I/O map is one default field, replaced as a whole by a local value.
Collection options without job- apply to the subtree; job-prefixed variants
provide individual Job defaults. Every aggregate ancestor remains binding.
These controls do not provide a filesystem space quota or admission priority.
.Sh TERMINALS
.Cm attach
connects to an existing PTY job.
All attached clients see its screen and may type simultaneously.
Input may interleave; there is no input owner or line locking.
The job continues when a client disconnects, including an SSH disconnect.
.Pp
Press Ctrl-] followed by d to detach.
Press Ctrl-] twice to send a literal Ctrl-].
.Pp
.Fl -detach-key Ar key
selects another detach key for this client:
.Cm ctrl-
followed by a letter or one of ], \e, ^ and _.
The second key stays d, and pressing the detach key twice still sends it once.
Without the option the key comes from
.Ev JOB_DETACH_KEY ,
then from detach_key in the terminal table of
.Xr job.conf 5 ,
then Ctrl-].
With
.Cm none
no key is intercepted: detaching is only possible by closing the client's own
terminal, which leaves the program running.
.Cm run Fl -pty
uses the environment and the configuration.
Ctrl-C, Ctrl-Z and Ctrl-D are forwarded to the shared terminal and follow
its current terminal settings. They may affect the program for every client.
.Pp
The shared window uses the smallest attached row and column dimensions.
With no clients the last size remains.
A new attachment receives the current visible screen.
Historical scrollback is not restored; use
.Cm log
for recorded output.
.Pp
Use the same Unix account and state directory when connecting over SSH.
Logging in under another account does not grant access to the service.
No separate application login is required.
A terminal survives a restart of the service alone.
A reboot, supervisor failure, or service stop that kills the whole process tree
ends it. It cannot be attached after the job finishes.
.Pp
Terminal emulation supports common text applications, not every xterm extension.
There is no clipboard forwarding or graphical terminal protocol.
Slow clients are disconnected and can reattach.
Output logs combine stdout and stderr; displayed controls are escaped.
Input is not separately recorded, but program output or terminal echo may reveal it.
.Sh QUEUES
.Cm queue add Ar name
creates a queue with no implicit concurrency restriction. The preferred spelling is
.Cm queue create Ar path .
.Cm queue set Ar name Ar settings
changes settings.
.Cm queue pause | resume Ar name
holds or releases waiting jobs; it does not freeze running processes.
.Cm queue clear Ar name
cancels waiting jobs.
.Cm queue rm Ar name Op Fl -when-empty
removes or drains a queue.
.Pp
Groups may contain both Queues and Groups. Use
.Cm group create Ar path
and
.Cm queue create Ar path .
Both accept
.Fl -max-running Ar number .
Group concurrency is counted across its subtree.
.Cm group pause | resume Ar path
controls admission while preserving child holds.
.Cm group close | open Ar path
controls new submissions.
.Pp
Both object types support
.Cm list , show , rename , move , unset , close , open , remove .
Use
.Cm move Fl -group Ar destination Ar path
for an inactive object. IDs survive path changes; cycles and duplicate
sibling names are refused. Removal refuses nonempty containers and Job records.
.Cm show Ar path Fl -json
reports local and inherited configuration.
.Pp
An empty Queue adds no local configuration or serial execution requirement.
The ordinary service profile uses only declared resource requests.
Legacy estimation, backfilling and host protections require the explicit
legacy profile described in
.Xr job.conf 5 .
.Sh JOB LIFECYCLE
.Cm create Op options
.Fl - Ar command ...
creates held work and prints its ID without starting it.
.Cm edit Ar id Op options
.Fl - Ar command ...
replaces the complete requested command, options and environment of a held or
queued Job. The original submission and Job ID remain unchanged.
Active and terminal Jobs cannot be edited.
.Cm release Ar id
releases held work to scheduling. Release requires an open Queue;
a Queue pause still prevents execution.
.Pp
Status distinguishes held, queued, starting, running, suspended, stopping and terminal
outcomes: succeeded, failed, cancelled and lost. Exit status and failure
reasons remain separate fields. Cancellation before execution records no
start time. Stopping work retains its resource accounting until completion.
The stopping deadline survives a service restart.
.Pp
Original submitted, latest requested and effective launch specifications are
recorded separately. Original and current environments are retained in private
state files and are not included in status output.
.Pp
.Cm retry Ar id
.Op Fl -hold
.Op Fl -current-env
.Op Fl -queue Ar path
.Op Fl -allow-lost
.Op Fl -json
creates the next attempt of a completed Job under its existing ID.
The previous attempt is archived atomically with publication of the next.
The saved environment is reused unless
.Fl -current-env
is given. Current Queue defaults are resolved again from the latest requested
specification. A renamed Queue is followed by identity; a removed Queue
requires an explicit destination. Closed Queues reject retries.
.Fl -hold
permits inspection and editing before release. Retry enters admission at the
back of the current arrival order; it does not inherit the old waiting age.
.Pp
.Cm attempts Ar id Op Fl -json
lists completed attempts and the current one. Structured output includes
schema_version and an attempts array. Results, specifications and timestamps
are retained separately per attempt. Environments are not returned.
.Cm log Ar id Fl -attempt Ar number
selects a retained historical log. Without this option, commands address
the current attempt. Logs remain subject to retention.
.Pp
Retry waits briefly for a completing supervisor to exit and refuses a
populated workload cgroup. Lost work requires
.Fl -allow-lost
because untracked processes or external side effects may remain.
This flag does not terminate old work or guarantee exactly-once execution.
.Sh REMOVAL
.Cm remove Ar id
.Op Fl -dry-run
.Op Fl -allow-lost
.Op Fl -json
removes a completed Job and every attempt, retained log, saved environment,
derived output template and estimator sample attributed to its identity.
Work-directory files remain. Unattributed legacy estimator samples, including
their command keys, remain; previews report their count.
.Pp
.Cm queue remove Ar path
and
.Cm group remove Ar path
accept the same options and
.Fl -recursive .
The default refuses nonempty containers. Recursive removal selects the mixed
subtree and Jobs currently belonging to its Queues. It refuses held, queued
and active Jobs; cancellation is a separate operation. Root and default Queue
cannot be removed. Historical object identities remain available to surviving
attempt references. A Job retried into another Queue is not selected merely
because its earlier attempt used a removed Queue.
.Pp
Dry run is read-only and returns a versioned selection with readiness and
blocking reasons. A successful dry-run exit means the preview was produced,
not that the operation is ready. Before mutation the CLI revalidates the
selected object identities and Job attempts. Stale selections fail.
Any lost attempt requires explicit
.Fl -allow-lost ;
this never overrides a known live supervisor or populated workload cgroup.
.Pp
Successful removal is quiet unless JSON output is requested.
The service records a private operation journal before retiring data.
Interrupted cleanup is retried and completed at startup. Exit 75 can mean
that a supervisor is still exiting or an accepted cleanup remains pending;
structured output distinguishes these cases. Other administrative changes
are refused while committed cleanup is blocked. Existing execution continues
to be supervised. Minimal receipts remain in the state directory's removals
subdirectory, and retired Job IDs are never reused.
.Sh RECURSIVE CANCELLATION
.Cm queue cancel Fl -recursive Ar path
and
.Cm group cancel Fl -recursive Ar path
capture the current nonterminal Job attempts in the selected subtree.
.Fl -dry-run
reports the selection without changing it;
.Fl -json
produces a versioned selection or operation with per-Job results.
New arrivals after selection are excluded. Changed attempt or membership
invalidates an uncommitted selection. Natural completion is a successful no-op.
.Pp
The service saves a cancellation operation before acting and blocks admission
of its captured attempts until application is recorded. Partial failures
remain pending and are retried, including after restart. Held and queued Jobs
become cancelled without starting. Active Jobs enter stopping, are thawed
if necessary, and receive graceful termination followed by escalation under
the existing termination deadline. An accepted operation does not mean
all processes have exited; use
.Cm wait
to observe completion.
.Pp
Exit 75 means application or recording remains pending. Exit 1 means a
completed operation contains a per-Job error. Exit 0 means application was
recorded for every selected attempt. Collection configuration and admission
holds are unchanged. Cancellation never removes Job records. Minimal
cancellation operation records survive separate removal and are retained
for recovery and replay. Offline backup and migration require their completion.
.Sh SUSPENSION
.Cm suspend Ar id
.Op Fl -timeout Ar duration
.Op Fl -json
requests cgroup v2 freezing of a local workload and confirms the kernel state.
.Cm continue Ar id
uses the same options and requests thawing.
Neither operation substitutes process signals when cgroup freezing is unavailable.
Remote execution must be controlled on its execution host.
.Pp
Success confirms the effective freezer state. A suspended Job retains memory,
open files, reservations and its concurrency slot. Elapsed deadlines continue.
Cancellation requests thaw before TERM, with the existing escalation deadline.
No application checkpoint is created.
.Pp
Confirmation waits five seconds by default, at most sixty seconds.
Exit 75 means the saved request is still pending; a timeout does not undo it.
An ancestor frozen by the administrator can prevent continuation.
Inspect suspension.requested, suspension.pending and suspension.error in
.Cm status Ar id Fl -json .
An accepted request remains active after a kernel-control error;
another explicit control replaces it.
Opposing controls are identified by generation while a caller waits.
.Pp
.Cm queue suspend Fl -recursive Ar path
and
.Cm group suspend Fl -recursive Ar path
select the currently active Jobs in the collection or mixed subtree.
Replace suspend with continue to thaw them. These commands accept timeout
and JSON options and return a result for each Job. Partial failures are
reported and produce a nonzero exit status. New admissions and independent
Queue pauses are unaffected. Use pause/resume for admission control.
.Pp
Structured timing separates elapsed_ms, suspended_ms and active_ms.
Active duration excludes observed frozen intervals; it is not CPU usage.
These are sampled wall-clock measurements. Requested suspension and observed
intervals survive service restart with the original cgroup backend.
.Sh SIGNALS
.Cm signal Op Fl s Ar signal
.Ar id
sends a signal to current workload processes, excluding the supervisor.
The default is TERM. Common signal names, names prefixed by SIG, and numeric
Linux signals are accepted. Success produces no output.
Processes that exit during delivery are skipped. An error may follow
partial delivery. Signals do not request cancellation or claim whole-Job
suspension. Use cancel for managed termination.
.Pp
Process control requires Linux pidfds. Recovery checks the boot identity
and process start time before adopting a supervisor.
.Sh ATTACHED RUNS AND DETACHED SUBMISSIONS
.Cm submit
leaves no client behind, so nothing that happens to a terminal reaches the Job.
.Cm run ,
.Cm wait ,
.Cm logs Fl -follow
and
.Cm attach
keep a client, and what happens to that client is defined as follows.
Only
.Cm cancel ,
or
.Cm run Fl -on-interrupt Cm cancel ,
cancels a Job; a client that stops never does.
.Bl -tag -width "closed"
.It first SIGINT or SIGTERM
.Cm run
in pipe mode forwards it to the processes of the Job as the same signal and
goes on waiting.
.Cm run Fl -pty
and
.Cm attach
detach and return 0; the Job goes on.
.Cm wait
and
.Cm logs Fl -follow
end by the signal; the Job is untouched.
.It second SIGINT or SIGTERM
.Cm run
in pipe mode returns 75; the Job goes on.
.It SIGHUP
.Cm run
in pipe mode returns 75; the Job goes on.
.Cm run Fl -pty
and
.Cm attach
detach; the Job goes on.
.Cm wait
and
.Cm logs Fl -follow
end by the signal; the Job is untouched.
.It closed output (EPIPE)
.Cm run
in pipe mode returns 75; the Job goes on.
.Cm run Fl -pty
and
.Cm attach
report the lost connection and return 125; the Job goes on.
.Cm logs
ends with 125;
.Cm wait
prints nothing and is unaffected.
Every other command that prints ends with 125 and without a message.
.It lost service connection
.Cm run
in pipe mode still reads the output from the record and returns 75 after 60
seconds without the service.
.Cm run Fl -pty
and
.Cm attach
return 125; attach again.
.Cm wait
tries again for up to 60 seconds or its timeout and then returns 75 with
outcome unavailable.
.Cm logs
reads the record and needs no service.
In no case is the Job cancelled.
.El
.Pp
With
.Fl -pty
the terminal is in raw mode, so Ctrl-C typed there is input for the Job and
no signal to the client; the detach key leaves the terminal.
.Pp
.Fl -on-interrupt
chooses what the first SIGINT or SIGTERM does to
.Cm run
in pipe mode:
.Cm forward ,
the default, as in the table;
.Cm detach
returns 75 at once and leaves the Job running;
.Cm cancel
requests cancellation and goes on waiting, so that the client returns 1 once
the Job has ended.
A second signal always returns 75 without waiting.
When the Job is not running yet, a forwarded signal reaches nothing, and the
client says so.
Whenever the client leaves a Job behind it prints the number of the Job and
how to wait for it and cancel it on standard error;
.Cm run Fl -json
then prints outcome
.Li detached
with exit_status 75 and job_id.
Under
.Ev JOB_CLI_COMPAT Ns = Ns Cm legacy
and without
.Fl -on-interrupt ,
.Cm run
installs no handlers: a signal ends the client and the Job goes on.
.Sh STANDARD INPUT
A Job in pipe mode reads end of file on standard input: its input is
.Pa /dev/null .
.Cm run Fl -stdin
instead passes the standard input of the client to the Job and closes it when
the client reaches end of file or ends.
The input is not recorded.
If no client connects within ten seconds of the start, for example because
the client was gone while the Job waited or because a later attempt was
started by
.Cm retry ,
the Job reads end of file.
.Fl -stdin
is refused together with
.Fl -pty ,
where the terminal is the input, and with
.Fl -on .
.Cm submit ,
.Cm create
and
.Cm edit
do not take it.
.Sh LABELS
.Fl -label Ar key=value
on
.Cm run ,
.Cm submit ,
.Cm create
and
.Cm edit ,
and on
.Cm queue | group create | set ,
attaches labels to a Job, Queue or Group.
An object carries at most 32 labels; a key has 1 to 63 characters from a-z,
0-9, dot, underscore and dash, and a value has at most 255 bytes without
control characters.
Labels classify.
They never affect scheduling, limits or any other policy, and a Queue or
Group does not pass its labels on to what it contains.
.Cm queue | group unset Ar path Cm label- Ns Ar key
removes one label and
.Cm unset Ar path Cm labels
removes all of them;
.Cm set
adds to the labels already there.
Labels appear in
.Cm show ,
.Cm status
and
.Cm list
and in every JSON record.
.Cm list Fl -label Ar key=value
and
.Cm queue | group list Fl -label Ar key=value
keep the entries that carry the label; several must all match.
.Sh LISTING JOBS
.Cm list
without options prints the held, queued and running Jobs as before.
.Fl -all
includes completed Jobs, and
.Fl -state
selects among held, queued, starting, running, suspended, stopping, succeeded,
failed, cancelled and lost.
The most recent 200 matching Jobs are listed unless
.Fl -limit
says otherwise, up to 10000; when more match, the listing says so on standard
error and sets
.Li more
in JSON.
With any of these options, or
.Fl -label ,
the text form is a table with the columns ID, STATE, QUEUE, SESSION, EXIT,
LABELS and COMMAND.
.Fl -format Cm tsv
prints a header line and one line per Job with tab-separated columns in this
order: id, attempt, state, queue, session, priority, submitted_ms, started_ms,
finished_ms, exit_status, labels, command.
An absent value is empty; labels is a JSON object and command a JSON array,
so no column holds a tab.
.Cm queue list
and
.Cm group list
print id, path, kind, paused, closed and labels in the same way.
Column names and state names are identifiers and are not translated.
.Sh JOB SETS
.Cm cancel , remove , release , retry , suspend , continue , signal ,
.Cm reprioritize , move
and
.Cm wait
take a set of Jobs: an ID, an inclusive range
.Ar first Ns Li - Ns Ar last ,
a comma list of IDs and ranges such as
.Li 1,4,7-9 ,
or several such operands, which are united.
There is no open range.
A reversed range, an empty item, zero, anything that is not digits, and a set
that names more than 10000 IDs are usage errors, and nothing happens.
.Pp
The service resolves the set in one request.
An ID inside a range that does not exist is skipped; an ID named on its own
that does not exist is reported.
A Job that cannot take the action is reported with the reason and does not
stop the others.
One plain ID answers as it always did.
A set answers one line per ID, or one summary line when more than 20 IDs
behaved the same, and with
.Fl -json
one object with
.Li schema_version ,
.Li kind
.Pq Li job_set ,
.Li action ,
.Li set ,
.Li selected
and one entry of
.Li results
per ID with
.Li id ,
.Li outcome ,
.Li result
and, where there is one,
.Li reason .
.Pp
The exit status is 0 when every selected existing Job took the action, 1 when
one was refused or failed, an ID named on its own does not exist, or the set
selects no existing Job, and 75 when a change is still pending.
.Fl -dry-run
answers what would happen to each Job and changes nothing.
.Pp
.Cm cancel
records the set as one cancellation operation, which a restarted service
completes;
.Cm remove
removes the completed Jobs of the set in one transaction.
The other commands are done Job by Job in ID order inside the service.
.Cm wait
waits for every Job of the set within
.Fl -timeout
and returns the first status in ID order that is not 0.
.Cm list Fl -id Ar set
lists the Jobs of a set, in every state unless
.Fl -state
says otherwise.
.Cm audit
shows one record per set request with the set as its target and the count of
each result.
.Sh MOVING A WAITING JOB
.Cm move Ar id Fl -queue Ar path
puts a held or queued Job into another Queue.
The destination must exist and be open, and its defaults, constraints and
priority bounds are applied and checked as at submission.
The Job keeps its number, attempt, submission time and accumulated waiting
credit; a move never adds credit.
A starting, running or ended Job is refused with a message that names its
state.
Every request is recorded by
.Cm audit
under the action move.
.Cm edit
remains the way to replace the command itself.
.Sh EXPLAINING SETTINGS
.Cm explain Ar id
says why a Job waits.
When a change of policy leaves a queued Job unable to start, for example a
Group budget lowered below what the Job asks for, the reason names the
resource and the object that holds the budget.
.Pp
.Cm explain Ar path Op Ar key
explains the settings of a Queue or Group, one of them or all: the value
configured there, the value in effect and the object that supplies it.
A key is an option name without dashes, such as memory-max or job-cpu-limit.
Where a setting cannot take effect as written, the reason follows: an
ancestor sets a lower ceiling, an explicit unlimited is still bounded by an
ancestor, a default for each Job exceeds what the subtree may hold together, a
default request exceeds an admission budget above it, or the host lacks the
cgroup controller.
.Fl -json
prints the same with key, option, configured, effective, source, takes_effect
and reasons for each setting.
.Sh RELIABILITY
A submission the service has answered is on disk and survives a service crash.
A client that lost its connection before the answer repeats the request with
.Fl -idempotency-key
and receives the same Job.
No command runs before its supervisor's identity is recorded; a launch
interrupted before that point is queued again and runs once.
The exit status is recorded when the command exits, before cleanup.
.Pp
The outcome lost means the supervisor confirmed the launch and ended, and
neither a result nor an exit status was found; the command may have run in
part or completely. Effects of a command outside job are never exactly-once.
.Pp
The key is compared with a digest of what was asked for: the command, the
working directory, the Queue, held or not, whether
.Fl -pty
was given, and every option that was given.
The terminal size, the session label, environment values and the user and
password of a proxy address are not part of it.
.Cm edit
of a held or queued Job with a key moves the key to the edited specification.
.Pp
.Cm audit
reads the journal of mutating requests in audit/ below the state directory,
without contacting the service. Each record has seq, at_ms, action, target,
peer_uid, peer_pid, session, params, result and operation.
An intent line is written before a change and a result line after it;
a change whose result line is missing is shown as
.Dq outcome unknown .
A request whose intent cannot be written is refused.
The arguments of a command are not recorded, because they often carry
secrets: the record holds the program name, the number of arguments and the
SHA-256 of the argument list.
Environment values and the user and password of any address inside a string
are not recorded.
Requests to attach, refused peers and unreadable requests are recorded, the
last two at most 30 times a minute.
.Fl -target ,
.Fl -action
and
.Fl -since
select records;
.Fl -json
prints one object per line with schema_version.
The journal rotates at 8 MiB and keeps eight files; the configuration table
.Li [audit]
sets
.Li keep_files
and
.Li rotate_bytes .
.Pp
Client and service state the request protocol range they speak. A request
outside the service's range, or a changing request without a version, is
refused; the client names both ranges and exits 125.
The service decides from the envelope before it reads the request inside it.
.Sh OPERATIONS
.Cm events
reads the journal of state changes in events/ below the state directory,
without contacting the service. There is one record for every transition of a
Job: held, queued, starting, running, suspended, stopping, succeeded, failed,
cancelled and lost, and requeued when a Job returns to its Queue after an
attempt or an unconfirmed launch. Each record has seq, at_ms, kind, job,
attempt, queue_id, queue_path, from, to, reason, exit_code, signal and, on
starting, wait_ms. Records of kind queue, group or host describe admission:
paused and resumed, closed and open, pressure_hold, pressure_recovering and
pressure_open, with object_id and object_path.
.Fl -job ,
.Fl -queue
and
.Fl -since
select records;
.Fl -queue
includes everything below the path.
.Fl -format Cm json
prints one object per line with schema_version.
.Fl -follow
keeps printing new records until interrupted; it looks for new records five
times a second and goes on across rotation.
The journal rotates at 8 MiB and keeps eight files; the configuration table
.Li [events]
sets
.Li keep_files
and
.Li rotate_bytes .
A record is written after the state it describes is on disk. It is forced
to disk with every final state of a Job, every admission record and every
rotation; a host crash can lose the records after the last of those, and a
line cut short is skipped and counted.
.Pp
.Cm queue show Ar path
and
.Cm group show Ar path
add the depth of the subtree: how many Jobs are held, queued, starting,
running, suspended and stopping, how long the oldest queued Job has waited,
and, for the Jobs started in the last hour, their number and the median and
longest wait. The wait statistics use at most the 4096 most recent starts
the service knows.
.Pp
.Cm host
adds a health section: uptime, state schema, protocol, the number of Jobs in
each state, how many supervisors the last start adopted and how many records
its recovery changed, the space left in the state directory, whether the
audit and event journals can be written, and whether limits are enforced or
only monitored. It also reports
.Li surrounding_limits :
the tightest memory.max, memory.high, cpu.max and pids.max among the cgroup
of the service and its ancestors, with the cgroup that sets each, the
effective CPU set, and the resource limits of the service itself.
.Cm doctor
checks all three as health, enforcement and surrounding_limits.
.Cm host Fl -json
also carries
.Li activity :
sync_calls and job_record_writes of the service process since it started,
starts_pending while admitted Jobs wait for their turn to be started, and
last_change with what the last changing request itself wrote. health counts
starter_failures, cancellations_failing and cancellation_retries.
.Pp
The service starts one Job at a time and answers other requests between two
starts. A request that lets many Jobs start returns at once; each of those
Jobs is shown as queued, waiting for its turn to start, until it is started.
In
.Cm status Fl -json
admitted_ms is the time the Job was admitted and started_ms the time its
command started, as its supervisor recorded it; started_ms is absent until
the service has read it and never changes afterwards. Run time counts from
started_ms, waiting time ends at admitted_ms.
.Pp
A service without a delegated cgroup runs in monitoring mode and its answers
say limits watched, not enforced. There, with the ordinary profile, a Job
that asks for
.Fl -mem ,
.Fl -pids
or a cgroup control is refused with a capability error. See
.Xr jobd 8 .
.Pp
A member of the socket group who cannot read the state directory gets the
output of
.Cm run ,
.Cm logs
and
.Cm log
from the service, and
.Cm attach
reaches the terminal through the runtime directory.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Cm config check Ar file
validates a TOML configuration without contacting the service.
.Cm config init Fl -profile Cm ordinary | legacy Ar file
creates a configuration without overwriting an existing file.
.Cm config show Op Fl -json
prints the running effective configuration and source as JSON.
.Cm config reload
requires drained Jobs and preserves the service profile.
Changing profile requires a drained restart.
.Sh AUTOMATION
Three commands serve a program that runs shell commands on somebody's behalf.
They are left out of
.Cm job help .
.Pp
.Cm classify
reads one command line, or one JSON object with command, per line of standard
input and prints direct, or route, a tab and the reason, for each.
A line is direct when it runs only file, text and query programs, most git
commands and job itself.
.Pp
.Cm policy Op Ar name
prints allow, or deny, a tab and the name of the rule, for each line of
standard input, by the command policy in
.Pa policy.json ;
see
.Xr job.conf 5 .
A line is a command, or a JSON object with command and optionally cwd and
caller_name.
.Ar name
is the name of the caller for lines that carry none.
.Cm policy Fl -show
prints the policy file in use and the rules in force and reads no commands.
Without a policy file nothing is forbidden.
.Pp
.Cm hook
reads one JSON object on standard input and writes one on standard output.
The question has command and optionally cwd, caller, caller_name and the
boolean detached.
The answer has decision, one of allow, deny and rewrite; reason with deny and
rewrite; rule with a denial by the command policy; and with rewrite the
command to run instead and detach, which is true when that command is meant
to be started without waiting for it.
A command the policy forbids is denied.
A command that
.Cm classify
calls route, and a
.Cm run
or
.Cm wait
that would hold its caller, is rewritten to a
.Cm run
with
.Fl -summary .
Nothing is executed.
Input that is not a JSON object with a command ends with status 125.
.Sh DOCTOR
.Cm doctor
checks this host and the service setup and prints one line per check:
.Cm ok ,
.Cm warn
or
.Cm fail ,
the name of the check, what was found and, where something is to be done,
what to do.
It runs without the service, changes nothing and takes no lock.
.Fl -system
reports on the system service.
With the service running it also reports the cgroup root the service chose
and compares the service's version and protocol with its own.
The checks cover the file locations, the configuration, the state directory
and its schema, the service lock and socket, access through a socket group,
cgroup v2 delegation and controllers, the freezer, pressure files, user
namespaces, Landlock, seccomp, capabilities, process handles, the network
tools, the service manager and, for a systemd user service, lingering.
.Pp
.Fl -json
prints an object with
.Cm schema_version
1 and an array
.Cm checks
of objects with
.Cm name ,
.Cm status ,
.Cm detail
and
.Cm fix ,
which is null when nothing is to be done.
The exit status is 0 when no check failed and 1 otherwise; a service that
does not answer is a failed check.
.Sh STATE MAINTENANCE
State maintenance requires the service to be stopped.
.Cm state validate Fl -source Ar directory
prints a JSON inventory and readiness report.
.Cm state backup Fl -source Ar directory Fl -destination Ar backup
creates a verified backup of the state of a stopped service.
Held and queued Jobs are copied as they are and wait again on the restored
service.
Records of Jobs that are starting, running, suspended or stopping are refused
unless
.Fl -interrupted
is given, which copies them as they are once their supervisors have ended;
the restored service records each of them as lost when it starts.
.Cm state migrate Fl -source Ar directory Fl -destination Ar destination
.Fl -backup Ar backup Fl -profile Cm ordinary | legacy
backs up the source and publishes converted state in a new directory.
Add
.Fl -dry-run
to print the inventory without copying or converting data.
.Pp
.Cm state restore Fl -source Ar backup Fl -destination Ar destination
verifies the backup and restores its original files into a new directory.
Backup and destination directories must not already exist or overlap the source.
Live network helpers, a pending cancellation and a pending resource update
prevent backup and conversion.
Conversion with
.Cm state migrate
also requires that no Job is held, queued or executing.
Maintenance never stops those processes.
The service lock may be created while inspecting state.
.Pp
Migration preserves original source data, Job IDs, outcomes and logs, and
converts legacy serial Queues into explicit concurrency settings.
Removed historical Queues receive retired identities.
Set
.Ev JOB_CONFIG
to the generated migration-config.toml when starting converted state.
Older binaries require the original backup format for rollback.
.Sh PROCESS PLACEMENT AND LIMITS
.Fl -cpu-affinity Ar LIST|inherit
selects a Linux CPU list, for example 0-3,8.
.Fl -numa-policy Ar inherit|default|local|bind:LIST|interleave:LIST|preferred:N
sets the task policy for future memory allocations. Node IDs are physical IDs.
Placement is inherited by children; it does not provide cpuset containment or
exclusive CPUs, and applications may change their own placement.
Explicit masks must be available and read back exactly at launch.
.Pp
.Fl -rlimit Ar NAME=SOFT:HARD
sets an inherited process resource limit. One value sets both soft and hard limits.
Each bound accepts inherit or unlimited; bytes accept binary K/M/G suffixes.
Resources as, core, data, fsize, memlock, msgqueue and stack use bytes;
cpu uses CPU seconds; rttime uses microseconds; other resources use integers.
Supported count/scheduling resources are nofile, nproc, sigpending, nice and rtprio.
The nproc, sigpending and msgqueue accounting scopes include the real user ID,
not only this Job. rss and locks are refused because modern Linux does not enforce them.
Hard-limit increases remain subject to operating-system privilege checks.
.Pp
Queue and Group defaults use --job-cpu-affinity, --job-numa-policy and --job-rlimit.
Each rlimit resource inherits separately. Use unset job-rlimit-nofile to remove
that local default, or nofile=inherit to preserve the supervisor's inherited values.
Profiles use cpu_affinity, numa_policy and rlimit_NAME={soft=VALUE,hard=VALUE}.
These settings affect future launches; update remains a cgroup-control operation.
Remote execution applies placement and rlimits at the destination, leaving the
local SSH transport unchanged. Host --json reports allowed IDs and inherited limits;
status --json records verified explicit launch controls in result.process_controls.
.Sh SECURITY CONTROLS
.Fl -no-new-privs Ar yes|inherit
sets the no_new_privs attribute before the program starts, so that neither it nor
its descendants gain privileges through set-user-ID, set-group-ID or file capabilities.
.Pp
.Fl -cap-drop Ar NAME,...|all|inherit
removes capabilities from the effective, permitted, inheritable and ambient sets.
The bounding set is reduced as well where the launch enters a user namespace of its
own or the service is privileged; result.security_controls.cap_bounding_reduced
records which. A service running as an ordinary user starts programs without
capabilities already.
.Pp
.Fl -seccomp-deny Ar NAME,...|inherit
installs a filter under which each named system call of the native ABI fails with
EPERM; clone3 fails with ENOSYS so that the C library falls back to clone.
Each name is one system call; related calls must be named separately.
A program of another ABI is killed when it makes a system call under a filter.
This is a deny list, not a sandbox.
.Pp
Capability reduction and a filter set no_new_privs and are refused together with
--no-new-privs inherit. Queue and Group defaults use --job-no-new-privs,
--job-cap-drop and --job-seccomp-deny; a Job may override them, and unset
job-seccomp-deny removes a local default. Profiles use no_new_privs, cap_drop and
seccomp_deny. Host --json lists supported names under security_controls.
Remote execution applies these controls, confinement and --net none at the
destination, leaving the local SSH transport unchanged.
These controls do not separate Jobs of the same Unix user from each other.
.Sh ISOLATION
.Fl -namespaces Ar LIST|inherit
starts the program in new namespaces of the named kinds: user, mount, pid, ipc,
uts and cgroup, separated by commas. Nothing is added to the list. A service
without privilege needs user in every list. The network namespace is chosen with
--net.
.Pp
With pid the Job runs in a PID namespace of its own and gets a fresh /proc, so
pid needs mount in the same list. The program sees only the Job's processes and
has a small process number; every process of the Job ends with the Job. Number 1
inside is job-init, a part of job that never executes another program. It starts
the command, reports its exit status or fatal signal unchanged, reaps orphans,
and forwards a signal sent to number 1 from inside to the command's process
group. Signal, cancel, suspend and continue reach the Job's processes as before,
and each signal arrives once. Processes left when the command exits are counted
and named in the result and then killed; helper programs otherwise kept end with
the namespace. job-init is one more process for --pids-max and --rlimit nproc.
Security controls, limits and confinement apply to the command and not to
job-init. status --json records proc, init_pid and command_pid under
result.isolation_controls.pid_namespace; host --json reports
pid_namespace_requestable and pid_namespace_error. A PID namespace hides
processes and is not a security boundary against the same Unix user.
.Pp
.Fl -root Ar read-only|inherit
makes every mount the Job sees read-only, including the working directory, /proc,
/sys and /dev/shm. It needs --namespaces user,mount.
.Pp
.Fl -writable Ar PATH,...|inherit
keeps up to 32 existing absolute paths writable under a read-only root.
It needs --root read-only. A path is resolved once when the Job starts; behind a
symbolic link the directory it leads to is made writable and recorded. With
--confine the writable paths are added to what the Job may write.
.Pp
.Fl -private-tmp Ar yes|inherit
gives the Job an empty /tmp and /var/tmp of its own, deleted when the Job ends.
It needs --namespaces user,mount. A writable path or a working directory under
/tmp or /var/tmp is refused with it. A private tmp that cannot be deleted is
moved to private-tmp-leftovers in the state directory and named in a note on the
result.
.Pp
A service that runs as root refuses --root read-only and --private-tmp yes unless
the Job drops the capability sys_admin (--cap-drop sys_admin or --cap-drop all),
because the Job could otherwise undo them.
.Pp
The namespaces are entered after the network setup and before confinement,
placement, limits and security controls. Before the program starts each requested
namespace, the read-only root, the writable paths and the private tmp are read
back; a step that fails is named in the start error and the program is not executed.
Queue and Group defaults use --job-namespaces, --job-root, --job-private-tmp and
--job-writable; a Job may override them, and unset job-namespaces removes a local
default. Profiles use namespaces, root, private_tmp and writable. Host and host
--json report what the host offers under isolation_controls; status --json records
what was applied in result.isolation_controls. Remote execution applies these
controls at the destination, leaving the local SSH transport unchanged.
A mount namespace is not a container, and these controls do not separate Jobs of
the same Unix user from each other.
.Sh OUTPUT QUOTAS
.Fl -output-head Ar SIZE
and
.Fl -output-tail Ar SIZE
set how much of the beginning and of the end of each recorded stream is kept.
SIZE takes K, M or G and lies between 1M and 1G; there is no unlimited value,
because an unbounded recording can fill the disk.
Queue and Group defaults use --job-output-head and --job-output-tail; a Job may
override them, and unset job-output-head or job-output-tail removes a local
default. Profiles use output_head_bytes and output_tail_bytes, and
.Xr job.conf 5
sets service defaults. Without any of these the recording keeps the first and
the last 32 MiB of all streams together, as before.
.Pp
The head is counted as stored, with 32 bytes per recorded chunk. The tail keeps
at least SIZE and at most 2 MiB more. Status prints the quota, the origin of
each value and the bytes removed per stream; with
.Fl -json
they are under spec.declared, resource_sources and result.output_retention.
Logs prints a notice at each gap naming the stream, the bytes removed and the
quota. A remote Job is recorded at the destination and again locally, each
with the quota resolved for it.
.Pp
The service removes whole recordings of completed attempts, oldest first, while
all recordings together exceed budget_bytes. A running Job's recording is
counted and never removed. A removed recording says so in logs and status.
.Sh NETWORK BOUNDARIES
.Fl -net Cm default
leaves the Job in the host's network.
.Fl -net Cm none
starts it in a network namespace of its own that holds only a loopback
interface: no IPv4, no IPv6, no name resolution.
.Pp
A proxy, a WireGuard tunnel or a bandwidth gives the Job a network namespace of
its own behind a filtering namespace. With a proxy, only TCP to the proxy's
address and port is forwarded; IPv6, UDP, ICMP and direct name resolution do not
leave the Job, and names are resolved by the proxy. With
.Cm wireguard:FILE
only the tunnel is forwarded, and name resolution uses the DNS lines of
.Ar FILE .
When the proxy or the peer stops answering, the Job has no network; nothing
falls back to a direct connection. The proxy variables the Job receives
(ALL_PROXY, and HTTP_PROXY and HTTPS_PROXY for an HTTP proxy) tell programs
where the proxy is; the boundary is the namespace, not the variables.
.Pp
A Job outside a Queue that sets a network or a bandwidth gets a filtering
namespace of its own, removed when it ends. The Jobs of a Queue that sets a
proxy, a tunnel or a bandwidth share one filtering namespace, one exit and one rate budget; each has its own
network namespace and address, and they cannot reach each other.
.Pp
A proxy's user and password are given in
.Fl -net-secret-file
or, for compatibility, inside the address. Every answer, listing, record and
log shows them as ***. The Job itself receives them in its proxy variables;
other clients of the service do not. Written inside the address they are
visible in the arguments of the submitting command while it runs, so prefer
the file. Inside the address they are USER:PASSWORD with @, / and spaces
percent-encoded. Records of Jobs that ended under an earlier version keep their
address on disk and are shown with ***; records of waiting and running Jobs are
rewritten when the service starts. An idempotency key ignores the user and
password. The service itself checks the proxy scheme and port and refuses
relative file paths. A Queue or Group takes
.Fl -net-secret-file
beside
.Fl -net .
The required tools are unshare, nsenter, sleep, ip, tc, nft and slirp4netns;
without them, or without user namespaces, such a Job is refused at submission.
.Pp
.Fl -net Cm ns:NAME
starts the Job in an existing network namespace.
.Ar NAME
is an entry under network.namespaces in the service configuration; a client
selects a name and never gives a path, and every admitted client can select
every name. The service checks at submission that the file is a network
namespace and that it can join it, and at launch that the process is in the
namespace the configuration names. A namespace made by root belongs to the
initial user namespace, and a service without that privilege is refused with
the kernel's reason; an ordinary-user service joins a namespace that its own
user made in a user namespace, named with user_namespace. Every Job that
selects the name shares the namespace with the others and with whatever else is
in it.
.Nm
adds no packet filter, name resolution or bandwidth limit there:
.Fl -bandwidth
and
.Fl -net-secret-file
together with it are refused.
.Pp
.Fl -net Cm profile:NAME
applies a routing profile from network.profiles in the service configuration:
one exit (the host, none, a proxy, a WireGuard tunnel or a configured
namespace), destination rules allow and deny for IPv4 addresses, ports and TCP
or UDP, resolver addresses, bandwidth, and sharing. Deny wins over allow, and
allow narrows what the exit forwards. With sharing per-job each Job has a
filtering namespace of its own; with per-queue the Jobs of one Queue share one,
and with shared all Jobs of the profile do. Jobs that share one keep a network
namespace each, cannot reach each other, and share the exit and the rate
budget. A Queue, a Group and a pinned execution profile can name a profile or
a namespace too. A Job's
.Fl -bandwidth
may be narrower than the profile's and not wider.
.Fl -net-secret-file
together with a profile that names its own secret file is refused.
The Job record keeps the name; the launch records the digest of the definition
and the rules it applied under network in
.Cm status Fl -json ,
and
.Cm explain
shows them. A Job whose profile was removed before it started ends with a
start error that names the profile.
.Pp
.Cm net list
prints the configured namespaces and profiles and whether this host can use
them;
.Cm net show Ar name
prints one with its rules and sharing.
.Cm host Fl -json
holds the same under network. Neither shows the content of a secret file.
.Sh OUTPUT RECORDING
.Cm logs Ar ID
reads a snapshot of retained output. Add
.Fl f
or
.Fl -follow
to follow until that attempt ends. Readers keep the selected attempt across retry.
.Fl -attempt Ar N
selects an earlier retained attempt.
.Fl -stream Ar all|stdout|stderr|combined|diagnostic
selects sources. PTYs and older recordings have no separate stdout or stderr.
Normal rendering escapes terminal controls and non-ASCII bytes; newline and tab remain.
.Fl -raw
preserves bytes.
.Fl -json
emits versioned JSON lines with sequence, capture time, source and byte arrays.
.Fl -since-ms Ar N
and
.Fl -until-ms Ar N
filter inclusive Unix epoch milliseconds. Older recordings have no timestamps.
.Fl -grep Ar TEXT
selects matching lines per source; lines exceeding 65536 bytes are processed as
bounded fragments. It cannot be combined with JSON.
.Pp
Retention keeps the first 32 and latest 32 one-MiB segments. Removed records are
reported as gaps; observation order across streams is not application write order.
A slow or disconnected reader does not stop collection. Native run uses raw
output; a closed output never cancels the Job:
.Cm run
returns 75 and
.Cm logs
ends with status 125.
A finite run budget returns 75 while the Job continues.
The first interrupt of a pipe-mode
.Cm run
is forwarded to the Job, see
.Sx ATTACHED RUNS AND DETACHED SUBMISSIONS ;
ending a
.Cm logs
or
.Cm wait
client does not signal the workload.
PTY attach continues to forward terminal input, including control-C.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width JOB_DETACH_KEY
.It Ev JOB_DETACH_KEY
Detach key of an attached terminal client, as for
.Fl -detach-key .
.El
.Bl -tag -width JOB_CGROUP_ROOT
.It Ev JOB_CONFIG
Explicit configuration path for the service.
.It Ev JOB_STATE_DIR
Select the state directory shared by service and clients.
The service socket is then daemon.sock inside it unless
.Ev JOB_RUNTIME_DIR
is set.
.It Ev JOB_RUNTIME_DIR
Directory of the service socket job.sock, shared by service and clients.
.It Ev JOB_CACHE_DIR
Cache directory; nothing is stored there yet.
.It Ev JOB_SYSTEM
The value 1 selects the system service and its locations, see
.Xr jobd 8 .
The option
.Fl -system
written before the command does the same for one invocation.
.It Ev XDG_STATE_HOME
Default parent of the job state directory; otherwise ~/.local/state is used.
When an exec store also exists, explicitly migrate it or select JOB_STATE_DIR.
.It Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Default parent of the socket directory job; without it the socket is
daemon.sock in the state directory.
.It Ev JOB_SESSION
Default descriptive session label; without it the label is unnamed.
.It Ev JOB_CLI_COMPAT
Absent or unix selects literal arguments and quiet wait.
The explicit value legacy preserves the previous single-argument Bash rule,
summary output and unwrapped wait JSON during CLI migration.
This is independent of the service's resource compatibility profile.
.It Ev JOB_CGROUP_ROOT
Delegated cgroup v2 tree, named explicitly. Without it the service discovers
the delegated cgroup it was started in, see
.Xr jobd 8 .
Without a usable tree, limits are watched rather than enforced by cgroups.
.It Ev JOB_FAILPOINT
For tests only: a comma-separated list of named points at which the service
or a supervisor ends itself or fails one write. Unset, it does nothing.
.El
.Sh HELP AND COMPLETION
Without arguments, and with
.Cm help
or
.Fl -help ,
.Nm
prints one screen of commands by section.
Every command accepts
.Fl -help
or
.Fl h
before the option delimiter and then prints its usage line, options, operands
and exit statuses without contacting the service.
.Cm help Ar command
prints the same,
.Cm help Fl -all
the long text of every part, and
.Cm help Fl -syntax
the syntax reference as Markdown.
Help, completion and the reference are produced from one command definition.
An option a command does not have is refused with one message that names the
option and the command.
.Cm log
and the earlier queue spellings keep their own reading of arguments.
.Pp
.Cm completion Cm bash | fish
prints a completion script.
It completes commands, options and listed values without the service.
Job identifiers and queue and group paths are asked from the service by a
query that gives up after about a third of a second and then offers nothing.
Commands that act on waiting or running work are offered the held, queued and
running jobs;
.Cm show ,
.Cm status ,
.Cm logs ,
.Cm log
and
.Cm attempts
are offered completed jobs as well, and
.Cm retry
and
.Cm remove
only completed ones, the most recent 200.
.Pp
.Cm list
prints the held, queued and running jobs,
.Fl -queue Ar path
those of one queue; see
.Sx LISTING JOBS .
.Cm show Ar id
is
.Cm status Ar id .
.Sh STRUCTURED OUTPUT
A command that accepts
.Fl -json
and prints one document also accepts
.Fl -format Cm json .
It prints one line holding an object with
.Li schema_version
1,
.Li kind
naming the command, and
.Li data
holding what
.Fl -json
prints.
.Fl -json
alone prints what it printed before.
When a command fails before it has a document,
.Cm run
and
.Cm wait
keep their result shape with
.Li outcome
.Li usage_error
or
.Li service_error ;
other commands print kind
.Li error
with
.Li command ,
.Li outcome ,
.Li exit_status
and
.Li error .
.Cm logs Fl -json
prints a stream of records, one object per line, and has no envelope.
.Cm audit Fl -json
is such a stream too;
.Cm audit Fl -format Cm json
prints one envelope whose data holds schema_version, entries and unreadable.
.Cm submit Fl -json
prints the Job number as before, and
.Cm submit Fl -format Cm json
wraps the Job record.
.Cm cancel ,
.Cm release ,
.Cm move ,
.Cm doctor
and
.Cm queue | group pause | resume
have an envelope as well.
A command that refuses its arguments before any request reaches the service
reports outcome
.Li usage_error ;
.Li service_error
means that the service was asked and refused or could not be reached.
.Cm list ,
.Cm queue list
and
.Cm group list
also take
.Fl -format Cm tsv .
.Pp
Output carries no colour and no terminal formatting, whether it goes to a
terminal or is redirected, and no option or environment variable changes
that.
The bytes a Job writes are passed on as they are.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width "128+S"
.It 0
Success.
.It N
.Cm run ,
.Cm wait ,
.Cm show ,
.Cm status
and
.Cm attach
return the exit status of the job itself.
.It 128+S
The job was ended by signal S.
.It 1
The job was cancelled or stopped by the service.
For
.Cm cancel ,
.Cm suspend ,
.Cm continue ,
.Cm remove ,
.Cm update ,
.Cm resource-update
and their queue and group forms: the request was refused or a member failed.
.It 75
A deadline passed while the job was pending or running, the service stayed
unavailable while waiting, the job shown is still pending or running, or a
requested change is still pending.
.Cm run
also returns 75 when its client left the job running after a second
interrupt, a hangup or a closed output.
.It 125
Service failure, lost job, start error, or usage error.
.El
.Pp
A job may itself exit with 1, 75 or 125; the outcome in the structured result of
.Cm run
and
.Cm wait
tells the cases apart.
Compatibility mode retains the prior status and output contract.
An attached client returns zero after a local detach; connection errors return
125 and the job may still be running.
Each command lists its own statuses under
.Fl -help .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
job submit --pty -- java -jar server.jar nogui
job attach 42

job run --pty -- bash
ssh -t user@host job attach 42

job log 42 tail 50
job cancel 42
.Ed
